WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:09.436
Japan, the early 1600s. The Tōkaidō runs from Edo to Kyoto, with a station about every ten kilometres.

00:00:10.136 --> 00:00:17.101
Each station has the same parts: an office that finds horses and porters, and a clerk who writes each one down.

00:00:17.801 --> 00:00:20.923
Inns, for every traveller, from lords to pilgrims.

00:00:21.623 --> 00:00:26.741
And each load goes one stage, to the next station, where it's moved onto fresh horses.

00:00:28.041 --> 00:00:37.014
But one stage was different: from Odawara to Mishima, about thirty kilometres over the Hakone mountains, the hardest stretch of the road.

00:00:38.114 --> 00:00:44.249
From the sea, the road climbs more than seven hundred metres and down again, with no station on the way.

00:00:45.549 --> 00:00:54.445
Everywhere else, fresh horses took over every ten kilometres or so. Here, the same horses and porters carried every load up and over the mountains.

00:00:55.945 --> 00:00:58.470
The road needed a station where it was hardest.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:10.845
In 1618, the shogunate founded one, at Hakone, by a lake on the mountain, and moved fifty households from Odawara and fifty from Mishima to run it.

00:01:12.145 --> 00:01:19.763
The hardest stage became two, of about fifteen kilometres each, and every load changed to fresh horses and porters halfway.

00:01:21.463 --> 00:01:27.642
At the utility, Ravi draws his data platform by its parts too, before he names a single product.

00:01:28.342 --> 00:01:37.143
Every part has something under it but one: nothing watches the platform itself, and its bill has gone up by half, with nobody able to say why.

00:01:38.243 --> 00:01:42.423
Drawn whole, the gap shows, and the platform gets the part it was missing.

00:01:49.123 --> 00:01:55.388
On Tomás's map, the data platform now sits in the technology layer, shared by every domain.

00:01:56.488 --> 00:02:02.388
Ravi opens it up and draws what it has to do, before what it's made of: a reference architecture.

00:02:03.688 --> 00:02:10.525
Products change. What a data platform has to do stays much the same, and drawn first, it shows what's missing.

00:02:13.225 --> 00:02:20.772
Four parts follow the data. Bring it in: from billing, the meters and the network map, as files, tables or streams.

00:02:22.072 --> 00:02:26.055
Store it, once, in an open format, where anyone allowed can read it.

00:02:27.255 --> 00:02:31.582
Process it: clean it, join it, and build each domain's data products.

00:02:32.682 --> 00:02:36.754
And serve it: to reports and dashboards, to apps, and to other systems.

00:02:38.054 --> 00:02:41.472
The Inner Life of Data follows one record through all four.

00:02:44.572 --> 00:02:46.512
Four more run across all of them.

00:02:47.212 --> 00:02:51.848
Catalogue and govern: what each table means, who owns it, and who may use it.

00:02:52.548 --> 00:02:56.581
Secure: who's asking, what they may reach, and every request checked.

00:02:57.281 --> 00:02:59.891
Orchestrate: what runs, in what order, and when.

00:03:00.591 --> 00:03:03.720
And observe: whether it all runs well, and what it costs.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:11.208
Then, under each part, the product that does it. Most are Databricks.

00:03:11.908 --> 00:03:18.912
Lakeflow Connect brings data in. The tables are kept in the cloud's storage, in Delta Lake, an open format.

00:03:19.612 --> 00:03:25.740
Lakeflow pipelines and dbt process it. Databricks SQL and the reporting tool serve it.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:30.861
Unity Catalog catalogues and governs. Lakeflow Jobs orchestrates.

00:03:31.561 --> 00:03:37.417
Secure takes three: the utility's identity provider, its network, and the catalogue's permissions.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:40.112
Under observe: nothing.

00:03:44.012 --> 00:03:51.082
The platform keeps its own records: every job's runs, every query, and what each one costs, in its system tables.

00:03:51.782 --> 00:03:57.628
Nobody reads them. Each domain watches its own data products, and nobody watches the whole.

00:03:58.928 --> 00:04:04.600
In September, finance asks, in a ticket, why the platform's bill is up by half since June.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.334
Nobody can say which jobs made it grow, or whose they are.

00:04:10.834 --> 00:04:16.493
So the only choices are poor ones: pay it, or cut every domain back, whether it grew or not.

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:27.050
Hakone's station was the highest on the road, more than seven hundred metres up, on empty ground by the lake.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:34.504
It was hard to keep going, and the shogunate took pains to keep it: the road's owner, for the whole road.

00:04:36.604 --> 00:04:43.002
Watching the platform is like that station: it serves every domain, so no one domain should have to build it.

00:04:44.702 --> 00:04:51.052
Each domain's logic belongs to the business. The platform's health and cost belong to the platform team.

00:04:54.952 --> 00:04:59.166
So Ravi's team builds the missing part, from the platform's own records.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:05.371
Every job and warehouse gets a tag with its domain, so the bill splits by domain.

00:05:06.871 --> 00:05:14.387
One dashboard shows every run, how long it took and what it cost, and a budget for each domain sends an alert when it's passed.

00:05:16.287 --> 00:05:24.252
In a week, it shows where the money went. One job had rebuilt three years of meter readings every night, not just the day's new ones.

00:05:26.352 --> 00:05:30.718
And a warehouse ran all night, for a dashboard nobody opened after six.

00:05:32.618 --> 00:05:36.077
The data team changes both, and reviews the pull requests.

00:05:37.777 --> 00:05:42.109
By November, the bill is back to June's, and finance closes its ticket.

00:05:43.809 --> 00:05:46.800
And each domain now sees its own share, every month.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:53.548
Here's the data practice, on Tomás's map.

00:05:54.848 --> 00:06:00.478
In the technology layer, the eight parts are the platform's services: what it does for every domain.

00:06:01.978 --> 00:06:09.940
Under them, the system software that realises each one: Databricks for most, the identity provider and the network for security.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.890
Drawn without the software, that's the reference architecture: any product can change under it.

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:25.749
And what was missing is a gap between the platform today and the one it should be, drawn on the map with the work that closes it.

00:06:29.249 --> 00:06:37.212
Hakone's station served the Tōkaidō for two and a half centuries, until the post-station system ended, in 1872.

00:06:37.912 --> 00:06:44.165
Ravi's platform has all eight parts now, each with something under it, and one of them watches the rest.

00:06:45.665 --> 00:06:47.702
Next: one platform, or many.
